1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,044
Lesson 1.6.

2
00:00:02,424 --> 00:00:03,436
Thermal noise.

3
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:08,211
Connect a very sensitive oscilloscope
to a resistor with no source

4
00:00:08,211 --> 00:00:11,318
at all and you will see a
voltage that never sits still.

5
00:00:11,698 --> 00:00:16,179
It is not a defect of the instrument: it
is the thermal motion of the electrons.

6
00:00:16,559 --> 00:00:19,968
That noise sets the floor of
every analog circuit and is

7
00:00:19,968 --> 00:00:23,317
precisely the raw material
of a thermodynamic computer.

8
00:00:23,697 --> 00:00:25,823
In this lesson we will quantify it.

9
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:30,137
Inside a conductor, electrons
collide endlessly with the

10
00:00:30,137 --> 00:00:32,982
lattice of atoms, which
vibrates at temperature T.

11
00:00:33,362 --> 00:00:37,637
At each instant, by pure chance, there
is a small excess of charge at one

12
00:00:37,637 --> 00:00:41,561
end or the other, and that produces
a voltage across the terminals.

13
00:00:41,941 --> 00:00:44,486
The hotter the material and the larger the

14
00:00:44,486 --> 00:00:47,030
resistance, the larger these fluctuations.

15
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:50,426
The average voltage is zero,
but its variance is not.

16
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:56,449
In 1928, Johnson measured this
noise and Nyquist explained it.

17
00:00:56,829 --> 00:01:01,611
The spectral density of the
open-circuit voltage is flat, that

18
00:01:01,611 --> 00:01:06,008
is, white noise, with value
4 k_B T R per unit bandwidth.

19
00:01:06,388 --> 00:01:12,209
The mean-square voltage in a
bandwidth Δf is 4 k_B T R Δf.

20
00:01:12,589 --> 00:01:16,645
Notice the structure: it is the
thermal energy, k_B T, times the

21
00:01:16,645 --> 00:01:20,448
resistance, which measures how
much the material dissipates.

22
00:01:20,828 --> 00:01:22,572
It is a first example of the

23
00:01:22,572 --> 00:01:26,622
fluctuation–dissipation theorem:
what dissipates also fluctuates.

24
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:30,928
Now let's put a capacitor in
parallel with the resistor.

25
00:01:31,308 --> 00:01:34,806
The capacitor filters out
high frequencies: the voltage

26
00:01:34,806 --> 00:01:38,049
becomes an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck
process, with memory.

27
00:01:38,429 --> 00:01:41,045
What you see is a
simulation of that process.

28
00:01:41,425 --> 00:01:46,408
The voltage histogram is Gaussian,
and its variance is k_B T/C.

29
00:01:46,788 --> 00:01:51,615
Let's change the resistance: with a large
resistor, the noise has more spectral

30
00:01:51,615 --> 00:01:55,769
density, but the filter is narrower;
with a small one, the opposite.

31
00:01:56,149 --> 00:01:58,697
The two effects cancel exactly.

32
00:01:59,077 --> 00:02:00,921
The variance does not depend on R.

33
00:02:02,050 --> 00:02:04,844
There is an immediate
way to reach k_B T/C.

34
00:02:05,224 --> 00:02:08,604
The energy of the capacitor is ½ C V².

35
00:02:08,984 --> 00:02:12,249
At equilibrium, the voltage
follows the Boltzmann

36
00:02:12,249 --> 00:02:15,981
distribution, which is a
Gaussian with variance k_B T/C.

37
00:02:16,361 --> 00:02:19,834
This is the equipartition
theorem: each quadratic degree of

38
00:02:19,834 --> 00:02:23,072
freedom holds, on average,
one half of k_B T of energy.

39
00:02:23,452 --> 00:02:28,726
We can also see it with the spectral
integral: 4 k_B T R times the

40
00:02:28,726 --> 00:02:34,239
filter's effective bandwidth, one
over four R C, again gives k_B T/C.

41
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:37,547
Let's run numbers at three hundred kelvin.

42
00:02:37,927 --> 00:02:42,342
A one-megaohm resistor measured
with a one-megahertz bandwidth has

43
00:02:42,342 --> 00:02:46,088
a noise of about one hundred
twenty-nine microvolts rms.

44
00:02:46,468 --> 00:02:49,918
A one-femtofarad capacitor,
typical of a node in a

45
00:02:49,918 --> 00:02:53,023
modern chip, fluctuates
about two millivolts.

46
00:02:53,403 --> 00:02:57,302
With one picofarad, the noise
drops to sixty-four microvolts.

47
00:02:57,682 --> 00:03:02,564
And the energy of a digital signal
on that one-femtofarad node, at 0.8

48
00:03:02,564 --> 00:03:07,306
volts, is about seventy-seven
thousand times k_B T: thermal noise is

49
00:03:07,306 --> 00:03:11,491
irrelevant to digital logic,
at the cost of a lot of energy.

50
00:03:12,617 --> 00:03:16,098
Let's compare the scales on a
single logarithmic energy ruler.

51
00:03:16,478 --> 00:03:19,198
At one end, k_B T and the Landauer bound.

52
00:03:19,578 --> 00:03:23,716
At the other, the energy of switching
a digital node, tens of thousands

53
00:03:23,716 --> 00:03:27,388
of times larger, which guarantees
that noise never flips a bit.

54
00:03:27,768 --> 00:03:33,419
Thermodynamic computing deliberately sits
close to k_B T: it designs devices where

55
00:03:33,419 --> 00:03:38,450
signal and noise are comparable, so
that noise does the work of sampling.

56
00:03:39,550 --> 00:03:44,455
In engineering, Johnson noise
limits sensors and amplifiers, and

57
00:03:44,455 --> 00:03:48,594
k_B T/C noise sets the
precision of analog converters.

58
00:03:48,974 --> 00:03:53,754
In thermodynamic computing, the same
noise drives the coupled oscillators that

59
00:03:53,754 --> 00:03:57,859
sample Gaussians and the pbits that
fluctuate between zero and one.

60
00:03:58,983 --> 00:04:04,258
In the next unit we will write the Langevin
equation, which describes exactly this

61
00:04:04,258 --> 00:04:08,760
noisy voltage; we will study the
Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process in detail,

62
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:13,682
and the fluctuation–dissipation theorem,
which generalizes Nyquist's formula.

63
00:04:14,783 --> 00:04:20,256
Common mistakes: believing a larger
resistor gives more total noise in an RC.

64
00:04:20,636 --> 00:04:23,112
Confusing spectral density with variance.

65
00:04:23,492 --> 00:04:27,781
Using Nyquist's formula at frequencies
where quantum mechanics matters.

66
00:04:28,161 --> 00:04:32,703
And confusing thermal noise with
shot noise or one-over-f noise.

67
00:04:33,817 --> 00:04:36,730
In summary: every
resistor at temperature T

68
00:04:36,730 --> 00:04:39,780
generates white noise
with density 4 k_B T R.

69
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:44,020
A capacitor at equilibrium
fluctuates with variance

70
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:47,730
k_B T/C, by equipartition
and independently of R.

71
00:04:48,110 --> 00:04:51,112
Digital chips move away
from k_B T to ignore

72
00:04:51,112 --> 00:04:54,319
noise; thermodynamic ones
move close to use it.

73
00:04:55,450 --> 00:05:00,784
To practice: compute the noise of a
fifty-ohm resistor over one gigahertz.

74
00:05:01,164 --> 00:05:03,955
Find the capacitance that
gives one millivolt of noise.

75
00:05:04,335 --> 00:05:08,055
Prove the spectral
integral of k_B T/C noise.

76
00:05:08,435 --> 00:05:11,551
And compare the energy of
a digital node with k_B T.
